{
  "comment": "Words in SuperEditr's windows and menus that the end-to-end UI drivers look for. `text` is what the app shows, the same on every platform or given per driver (`macos`, `linux`, `windows`) where the words differ. `made_by` says how the tests get it: `ui` renders the component in `file` (ui/src/lib/ui-wording.svelte.test.ts; with `role`, the text is the whole accessible name of an element with that role, otherwise it is somewhere in the page's text), `rust` calls the function or reads the config that makes it (src-tauri/src/ui_wording_contract.rs), and `source` finds `literal` as a whole string literal in `file`, with `vars` put in. Each of `matched_by` is a pattern a driver matches on the text, written exactly as it appears in `file`; `vars` are the driver's variables in it and the values they take here. `kind` is how the driver matches: `substring` (Swift `contains`), `regex` (Swift `.regularExpression`, grep -E), `regex-ignore-case` (PowerShell `-match`), `wildcard` (PowerShell `-like`, the whole text, ignoring case), `equals`, `prefix` (Swift `hasPrefix`), and `probe-button` (tests/ui/linux/probe.py's button name: `~rest` for a name containing rest, otherwise the whole name). src-tauri/src/ui_wording_contract.rs checks each pattern against `text` and finds it in its driver.",
